{# ONE GROUPED DROPDOWN OF THE HOUSE FILTER BAR.

   A CLOSED CHIP THAT OPENS A PANEL OF OPTIONS WITH LIVE COUNTS — the
   incidents pattern, which is the standing rule for every filter bar in the
   product.

   IT IS A `<details>`, AND EVERY OPTION IS A LINK. The address is the state,
   so a filtered board is a link somebody can send and a page a browser can go
   back to; and the whole control opens, chooses and clears with no script of
   ours, which is what keeps a filter bar working on a page that has none.

   THE COUNTS ARE OF THE WHOLE BOARD, not of what is already filtered: a count
   that moved as you filtered could not tell you what picking an option would
   do, which is the one thing it is for. #}
<details class="i-dd">
    <summary class="mchip i-ddt{{ chosen ? ' on' : '' }}">
        <span class="i-ddval">{{ chosen ?? all|raw }}</span>
        <span class="i-ddcaret">&#9662;</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="i-ddmenu">
        <span class="i-ddhead">{{ label|raw }}</span>
        <a class="i-ddopt{{ chosen ? '' : ' on' }}" href="{{ path(app.request.attributes.get('_route'), app.request.attributes.get('_route_params')|merge(query.with(key, null))) }}">
            <span class="i-ddopt-l">{{ all|raw }}</span>
        </a>
        <span class="i-ddsep"></span>
        {% for value, count in counts %}
            <a class="i-ddopt{{ chosen == value ? ' on' : '' }}"
               href="{{ path(app.request.attributes.get('_route'), app.request.attributes.get('_route_params')|merge(query.with(key, value))) }}">
                <span class="i-ddopt-l">{{ value }}</span>
                {% if count > 0 %}<span class="i-ddopt-n">{{ count }}</span>{% endif %}
            </a>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
</details>
